# Machine-local logs are records, not shared source. A 3-way merge that
# interleaves two machines' log lines is meaningless, so never content-merge
# them: keep the committing side's copy.
hardware/*/logs/**  -merge
logs/**             -merge

# NOTE: results*.jsonl is deliberately NOT `merge=union`, despite the #292
# proposal. scripts/exclude_rows.py annotates a row in place (adds `excluded`/
# `exclusion_reason` and atomically rewrites the file), and results.load() does
# not de-duplicate. A union merge of two diverged checkouts would therefore
# keep BOTH the original and the excluded copy of a rewritten row, and the
# un-excluded copy would re-enter every pass rate -- a deliberately archived row
# restored by the merge. Different machines write different
# hardware/<id>/results.jsonl paths and never conflict; the only union scenario
# is one machine committed from two checkouts, where the default 3-way merge
# conflicts loudly and a human reconciles -- which is what must happen when an
# exclusion is in the diff. See CONVENTIONS.md, "One main, machines are
# directories".
